---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Cmd from '../components/Cmd.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import { faqPick, mcpJson } from '../content/site';

const title = 'MCP memory server for Claude Code, Cursor and Windsurf | hippo';
const description =
  'hippo mcp runs a local memory server over stdio for any MCP client. Memories stay in SQLite on your machine, with no account and no API key. MCP access alone does not capture sessions.';

// README "MCP Server" names the 13 tools; each line paraphrases its description in src/surface/mcp/server.ts.
const tools = [
  { name: 'hippo_recall', does: 'Memories that match a query, ranked within a token budget.' },
  { name: 'hippo_assemble', does: "A session's context in time order, within a token budget." },
  { name: 'hippo_drill', does: 'Opens a topic summary and lists the memories under it.' },
  { name: 'hippo_remember', does: 'Stores a memory. Errors get twice the half-life, and pinned memories never decay.' },
  { name: 'hippo_outcome', does: 'Reports whether the recalled memories helped.' },
  { name: 'hippo_context', does: 'Works out the current task from git and returns matching memories.' },
  { name: 'hippo_status', does: 'Counts, strengths and when the store last slept.' },
  { name: 'hippo_learn', does: 'Scans recent git commits and stores the lessons.' },
  { name: 'hippo_conflicts', does: 'Lists memories that contradict each other.' },
  { name: 'hippo_resolve', does: 'Settles a conflict by keeping one memory.' },
  { name: 'hippo_share', does: 'Copies a memory to the global store for other projects.' },
  { name: 'hippo_peers', does: 'Lists the projects that feed the global store.' },
  { name: 'hippo_predict_baserate', does: 'Base rates for past predictions, to check a new one against.' },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="MCP server" published="2026-09-28">
  <Nav />
  <main id="main">
    <PageIntro>
      <Fragment slot="title">MCP memory server for <span class="text-gradient">Claude Code, Cursor and Windsurf</span></Fragment>
      <p>
        <code>hippo mcp</code> runs a memory server over stdio on your machine, with no account and no API key. Any MCP
        client can use it, including Claude Code, Cursor, Windsurf (now Devin Desktop), Cline and Claude Desktop. The
        agent gets 13 tools to recall and store memories, mark what it recalled as helpful or wrong, and see where
        memories contradict each other.
      </p>
    </PageIntro>

    <Section title="Add it to your client">
      <Cmd lines={['claude mcp add hippo-memory -- hippo mcp']} copy />
      <p>
        That is all Claude Code needs. Other clients take a config block. Cursor reads it from
        <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code>, Claude Desktop from <code>claude_desktop_config.json</code>, and Windsurf and Cline
        from their own MCP settings.
      </p>
      <Cmd lines={mcpJson} prompt={false} copy />
      <p>
        With no global install, <code>"command": "npx", "args": ["-y", "hippo-memory", "mcp"]</code> works too. If there
        is no store yet, the first tool call creates the global one in <code>~/.hippo</code>. The server gives an agent
        tools and captures nothing by itself, so run <code>hippo init</code> as well for the agents that support hooks.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The 13 tools" alt>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-white/8" data-scroll-x>
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
          <caption class="sr-only">Tools the hippo MCP server exposes</caption>
          <thead class="bg-white/[0.03] font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-3 font-medium sm:px-5">Tool</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-3 font-medium sm:px-5">What it does</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
            {tools.map((t) => (
              <tr class="align-top">
                {/* break points after underscores give the description column room on a phone */}
                <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-3 text-left font-mono text-xs font-normal text-accent-light sm:whitespace-nowrap sm:px-5">{t.name.split('_').map((part, i, all) => <Fragment>{part}{i < all.length - 1 && <Fragment>_<wbr /></Fragment>}</Fragment>)}</th>
                <td class="px-3 py-3 text-zinc-300 sm:px-5">{t.does}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Where the memories live" id="choosing-an-mcp-memory-server-in-2026">
      <p>
        The question to ask of any MCP memory server is where the memories live and who runs it. The servers from Mem0,
        Zep and Letta are hosted and need an account or an API key. In
        <a class="link" href="https://docs.mem0.ai/platform/mem0-mcp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">mem0's words<span class="sr-only"> (opens in new tab)</span></a>,
        memories stored that way "live in your Mem0 account, not on your computer". Hippo keeps them on your machine. It
        also adds the ability to mark a recalled memory wrong, replace an old fact and list contradictions.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="link" href="/vs/mem0/">hippo vs mem0</a>, <a class="link" href="/vs/zep/">hippo vs Zep</a>,
        <a class="link" href="/vs/letta/">hippo vs Letta</a>
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Faq
      items={faqPick(['Can I use hippo as an MCP memory server?'])}
      heading="MCP questions"
      moreHref="/faq/"
    />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
